"event_title"} "Flyweight, Greco-Roman (≤52 kg), Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "3 –  6 August 1948 — 10:00-13:30-18:00-23:30 (all days)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/341"} "Empress Hall, Earls Court Exhibition Centre, Kensington, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/122869"} "13 from 13 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Elimination by bad points.  Win by fall = 0 points; win by decision = 1 point; loss by 2-1 decision = 2 points; loss by 3-0 decision = 3 points; loss by fall = 3 points.  Three judges gave decisions, thus all decisions were either 2-1 or 3-0.  Five bad points eliminated a wrestler."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The 1947 European Champion was Sweden’s Bertil Sundin but he did not compete in London. This class was closely contested, with five wrestlers remaining after four rounds, all with between 2 to 4 bad points. Turkey’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/60356"} "Kenan Olcay"] " benefited from a bye in round five to advance to the final round, where he met Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/58935"} "Pietro Lombardi"] ", who had decisioned Sweden’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/60094"} "Malte Möller"] " in the fifth round. Lombardi then defeated Olcay by split decision to win the gold medal, with Olcay taking silver. The bronze went to Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/57899"} "Reino Kangasmäki"] " based on his second round victory over Möller, although Kangasmäki had won two and lost two matches."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Lombardi would compete at the " [:a {:href "/results/123502"} "1952 Olympics"] " without winning a medal, but won World Championship bronze medals as a bantamweight in 1950 and 1955."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Round Eliminated"] [:th {} "Ba